g conjecture of U Faigle and B Sands is proved: For every number R > 0 there exists a number n(R) such that if \\text{L} is a finite distributive lattice whose width w(\\text{L}) (size of the largest antichain) is at least n(R), then |\\text{L}| ⩾ Rw(\\text{L}). In words this says that as one considers increasingly large distributive lattices, the maximum sized antichain contains a vanishingly small proportion of the elements.\n\n^{☆}\n    \n\nSupported in part by NSF Grant MCS83-01867.\n\n^{**}\n    \n\nSupported in part by a Sloan Research Fellowship.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEXTENSIONS OF THE KAHN–SAKS INEQUALITY (https://www.math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/KS-v35.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Posets of width two serve a useful middle ground as on the one hand there are sufficiently many of them to retain the diversity of posets, and on the other hand they can be analyzed by direct combinatorial tools.In this paper, we study two classical results in the area: the *Stanley inequality* (1981), and its generalization, the *Kahn–Saks inequality* (1984). ... paper [CPP21a], where we established the cross-product conjecture in the special case of width two posets.Now, our approach allows us to obtain q-analogues of both inequalities in the style of the q-cross-product ... \\)(1.1)In other words, the distribution of value of linear extensions on \\(x\\) is log-concave.Date: February 3, 2022.\nBoth inequalities were proved using the geometric *Alexandrov–Fenchel inequalities* and remain largely mysterious. Despite much effort, no combinatorial proof of these inequalities has been found.We give a new, fully combinatorial, proof of the Kahn–Saks inequality for posets of width two. In this\ncase, linear extensions are in bijection with certain lattice paths, and we prove the inequality by explicit\ninjections. This is the approach first pioneered in [CFG80, GYY80] and more recently extended by the\nauthors in [CPP21a]. In fact, Chung, Fishburn and Graham [CFG80] proved Stanley’s inequality for width\ntwo posets and their conjecture paved a way to Stanley’s paper [Sta81]. The details of our approach are\nsomewhat different, but we do recover the Chung–Fishburn–Graham (CFG) injection as a special case.\nThe construction in this paper is quite a bit more technical and is heavily based on ideas in our previous\npaper [CPP21a], where we established the cross-product conjecture in the special case of width two posets.Now, our approach allows us to obtain q-analogues of both inequalities in the style of the q-cross-product\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProof of the Kahn Saks Conjecture | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2609.30895)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; We prove (Theorem 1) that sufficiently large width forces $δ(P)$ to be arbitrarily close to $1/2$, answering a long-standing conjecture of Kahn and Saks. ... Publish: 2026-09-25 07:04:00 UTC\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVariance vs. range for linear extensions, and balancing extensions in posets of bounded width (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26134)\nciteturn6academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; An old conjecture of Kahn and Saks says, roughly, that any poset $P$ of large enough width contains elements $x,y$ which are \"balanced\" in the sense that the probability that $x$ precedes $y$ in a uniformly random linear extension of $P$ is close to $1/2$. ... The implication follows from our two main results: first, that if $\\pi(P):=\\max \\pi(x)$ is large then $P$ has large variance, i.e. there is a $y$ whose position in a uniform extension of $P$ has large variance; and second, that the conclusion of the Kahn-Saks Conjecture holds for $P$ with large variance and bounded width.\n--------------------------